# SlidewareEngineering - Audio Presentations

## Covered Topics

### Arrays to Air (AirToArrays)
A technical deep-dive into digital audio fundamentals:

- **Basic tone properties**: Frequency (pitch), amplitude (volume), and how they map to perception
- **Sound as color**: Visual parallel between audio frequency and light wavelength
- **Human hearing range**: Speech, music, and equipment limitations (50Hz - 10kHz practical range)
- **Logarithmic perception**: Linear sliders feel unnatural; log scales match human hearing (Weber-Fechner law)
- **Loudness is psychoacoustic**: dBFS, equal-loudness contours (Fletcher-Munson), why same amplitude sounds different at different frequencies
- **Phase and interference**: Two tones interacting, constructive/destructive interference, beat frequencies
- **Waveforms and harmonics**: Sine vs square vs triangle vs sawtooth - each has different harmonic content visible in FFT
- **Custom waveforms**: Drawing arbitrary sample buffers, why time-domain manipulation is impractical
- **Fourier decomposition**: Any periodic signal can be represented as a sum of sine waves
- **Voice reconstruction**: Decomposing and reconstructing speech with oscillator banks
- **Spectrograms**: Time x frequency x magnitude plots for understanding complex signals

### What The Audio (WhatTheAudio)
An interactive demo collection focused on experiential learning:

- **Sound as color**: Visual mapping of frequency to visible light spectrum
- **Voice has a shape**: 3D FFT visualization of speech
- **Waveform shapes**: Hear how sine/square/triangle waves differ and see their harmonics
- **Audio effects**: Apply VHS, old phone, underwater filters to music/voice
- **Echo disruption**: Demonstrates how delayed audio feedback makes speaking nearly impossible

### Stop Copying Me (StopCopyingMe)
Deep dive into acoustic echo cancellation (AEC) for telephony:

- **Echo perception thresholds**: Self-feedback (<5ms), reverb (10-40ms), disruptive echo (>50ms)
- **Half-duplex**: Early speakerphone approach - mute mic when far-end is active. Attack/decay/threshold tuning. Poor conversation flow.
- **Naive subtraction**: Just subtract far-end from mic. Fails due to acoustic delay.
- **Time-aligned subtraction**: Use cross-correlation to estimate loopback delay, then subtract. Reduces comb effect but room coloration defeats simple subtraction.
- **Room Impulse Response (RIR)**: Measure room transfer function with test signals (Dirac, MLS). Convolve and subtract. Works but static measurement goes stale.
- **LMS adaptive filter**: Real AEC that continuously models the echo path. Covers step size, filter length, leakage, convergence. Discusses double-talk problem and non-linear distortion.
- **Playground**: All strategies combined for experimentation

